<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#u-boat">Glossary</a> · Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">U-boat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Unterseeboot</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">U-boat is the English name for German military submarines, from the German Unterseeboot. In both world wars U-boats waged campaigns against Allied merchant shipping that threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s supply lines, in 1917 and again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-atlantic">Battle of the Atlantic</a> of 1939–1945. Many were built at yards in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/u-boat.jpg" alt="U-boat" width="320" height="338" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Darkone; derivative work by Georgfotoart · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U995_2001_1_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word is an anglicized form of the German U-Boot, short for Unterseeboot, literally under-sea boat. Austro-Hungarian Navy submarines were also known as U-boats. German interest in submersibles began in 1848, when a conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> seemed likely and naval officers hoped a new weapon might offset the stronger Danish Navy. The three-man Brandtaucher, designed by Wilhelm Bauer in 1850 and built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a>, was lost during a test dive on 1 February 1851, and interest faded after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-schleswig-war">First Schleswig War</a>.</p><p>German navies received 181 submarine projects between 1861 and 1900, but only two practical submersibles were built in that time: a 5.3-metre boat by Otto Vogel in 1867, lost in trials in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, and a 14-metre boat at the Howaldt Yard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiel">Kiel</a> in 1897. Both had very limited capabilities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, on 5 September 1914, SM U-21 sank the light cruiser HMS Pathfinder, the first ship sunk by a submarine with a self-propelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a>, and on 22 September U-9 sank three armoured cruisers. Against merchant ships U-boats first observed the prize rules, which required them to stop a ship and take off the crew before sinking it. A campaign of unrestricted submarine warfare in 1915 was abandoned after strong American protests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> resumed it in February 1917, and it failed mainly because of the introduction of convoys and was a contributing factor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> joining the war.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> forbade German submarines, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a> ran a design office in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to keep up the technology. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the U-boat campaign was the major part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-atlantic">Battle of the Atlantic</a>, from 1939 to 1945. The wolfpack tactic against convoys brought spectacular results after August 1940, a time German submariners called the happy time. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-donitz">Karl Dönitz</a> the force pursued convoy battles, but improved convoy and anti-submarine tactics took a toll, and in May 1943, called Black May, losses began to outpace their effect on shipping.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> later wrote that the only thing that really frightened him during the war was the U-boat peril. The Type VII, the workhorse of the force, was completed in 709 boats in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and Paris Conferences of 1954, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>'s Bundesmarine was allowed from 1955 to commission submarines of up to 350 tons for coastal operations, and submarines of German design have since been built for other navies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “U-boat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the twentieth century Hamburg's shipyards, notably Blohm &amp; Voss, built warships such as the battleship Bismarck and many <b>U-boats</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the modern era its military importance lay in shipbuilding: the AG Weser and Bremer Vulkan yards built warships and <b>U-boats</b>, and during the Second World War forced labourers built the huge Valentin submarine bunker at Farge.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Free Hanseatic City of Bremen · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kriegsmarine’s <b>U-boat</b> campaign was defeated in the Atlantic, and after the Allied landings in Normandy in June 1944 the Wehrmacht was pressed back on every front.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Germany told the United States its <b>U-boats</b> would sink without warning any ship in the war zone from the next day.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-31">31 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
